<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwigskirche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1762">1762</a>–<a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ludwigskirche Saarbrücken</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ludwigskirche is a Lutheran church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarbrucken">Saarbrücken</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built between 1762 and 1775 to designs by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-joachim-stengel">Friedrich Joachim Stengel</a> for Prince William Henry of Nassau-Saarbrücken. Set in the Ludwigsplatz among matching palaces, it is one of the most important Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> churches in Germany. Burned out in 1944, it was rebuilt after the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwigskirche.jpg" alt="Ludwigskirche" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Staudt · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwigskirche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
